WEBVTT

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:29.980
If you take a look around you, one thing you will definitely notice is that...

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.520
virtually everything you see is man-made. Everything from buildings, clothes,

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:41.000
computers, to cars, roads, and everything in between. Humans have definitely learned

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.760
how to make our lives more comfortable, reliable, and safe through innovation.

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:49.280
Hi, I'm Jennifer Pulley. And I'm Johnny Alonzo, and today on NASA 360 we're gonna

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:53.120
take a look at how human ingenuity is making man-made objects stronger, safer,

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:55.060
and much more available for all of us.

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:12.630
For thousands of years, humans have taken objects from nature to create a better world for themselves.

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:17.050
Take, for instance, mud bricks from antiquity that were used to build dwellings.

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:24.290
Through trial and error, early carpenters learned that just building structures from plain mud often led to unstable structure.

00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:29.970
But when they combined mud and straw together, they came up with a new structure that resists both...

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:34.420
squeezing and tearing resulting in a much better dwelling although they may

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:38.300
not have known it these early builders were using composite materials even

00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:41.790
though the use of the term composite materials is generally synonymous with

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:46.860
space-age materials composites they've been around for a long time basically a

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:50.440
composite material is formed when you combine two or more items that have very

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:55.360
different properties many composites are made up of just two materials one that

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.940
acts like a glue to surround and bind and one to reinforce like fibers or

00:01:58.940 --> 00:01:59.320
fragments.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.880
When combined together, these different materials usually work together

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:06.300
to make the sum of the parts much better than the original materials alone.

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:11.260
Today, the use of new composite materials can be seen in virtually all space-age designs,

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:14.900
from new aircraft used by you and me, the general public,

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:18.420
to even next-generation spacecraft for future space missions.

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:22.810
Now, this turn to composites is because weight reduction combined with strength

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:27.070
has always been a critical goal flight, even since the beginning.

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:29.970
The first aircraft ever made were built from wood.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.300
and fabric material but it was soon realized that a major change needed to

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:38.480
be made to increase the strength and durability of aircraft. The change came

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:43.140
with the introduction of strong and lightweight metals like aluminum. Since

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:46.940
that time the use of these types of metals have been the state-of-the-art for

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:52.060
both aircraft and spacecraft. Today metal spacecraft are still state-of-the-art but

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:57.020
researchers have begun to look at new ways to build spacecraft that could offer

00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:58.340
for better alternatives to metal.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:02:59.960
In fact, NASA is already

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:04.490
testing what could be the next big step in spacecraft design right here at NASA

00:03:04.490 --> 00:03:10.560
Langley it's called a composite crew module or CCM I met up with NESC

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:14.180
principal engineer Mike Kirsch to find out a little more about this innovative

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:19.900
design like this CCM to me looks a little familiar I mean I'm thinking 60s

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:24.220
Apollo capsule it's very similar to the Apollo program many many shapes of work

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:28.480
for the mission that this was intended but in order to kind of streamline the

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:29.320
decision process.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.220
shape was picked. And that way we could leverage the aerodynamic data that we

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:37.390
collected during the Apollo program. So this is a slightly larger version of the

00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:42.080
Apollo. The Apollo is roughly 4.3 meters in diameter and this one's five

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:45.920
meters in diameter. If you recall Apollo had three astronauts on the inside. This

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:49.780
was designed to carry six, later lowered to carry four, but the volume is still

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:53.140
there to carry six crew to and from station. Mike we've been talking a little

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:58.260
bit about composite materials. What type of material is the CCM made of? This is a

00:03:58.260 --> 00:03:59.820
carbon graphite epoxy resin.

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:06.890
It's a fabric that was hand laid up on a male tool and then you put the entire system into

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:07.630
an oven.

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:12.290
It's actually a pressurized oven called an autoclave and it gets cooked and it comes

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:12.910
out hard.

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:17.840
Let me just kind of make sure I understand this, kind of like a wire mannequin wrapping

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:19.000
fabric around it.

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:19.920
Is that kind of what?

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:20.320
Yeah.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:20.480
Yeah.

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:20.880
Okay.

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:21.320
Okay.

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:23.380
So that's a very good analogy.

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:23.860
Okay.

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:25.520
And then that's only the first step though.

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:28.820
So after that we add what's called an aluminum honeycomb.

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:29.920
You glue the

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:35.160
honeycomb to the skin and you cure that and then after that you lay another skin

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:39.050
on top of the aluminum honeycomb and then you put that back in the oven and

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:43.700
you cure it and when it's all done it comes back as an assembly so you have a

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:49.100
thin skin right on the inside and an aluminum honeycomb in the center and

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:54.360
then another thin skin on the outside and it's rigid and cured just like that

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:57.650
and you know as you're describing this to me I'm thinking wow that's a lot of

00:04:57.650 --> 00:04:59.970
stuff but this is I mean I can barely feel it

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.440
It's very, very lightweight, which is the idea of composites.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:06.400
It's a high strength and stiffness to weight,

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:09.000
which is one of the objectives of the project.

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:13.200
The purpose of the project was to get some NASA guys

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:15.560
some hands-on experience designing, building,

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:16.720
and testing with composites.

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:20.100
Now, this is an alternative to the mainline program.

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:23.860
The mainline program, which is Constellation, in particular,

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:26.300
the Orion project, which makes the crew capsule.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:28.520
They chose aluminum lithium, which

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:29.740
which has very similar strength.

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:36.370
and stiffness to weight characteristics, but this is an alternative and by using composites

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:42.370
it enables complex shapes to be made and to give us a real world comparison between the

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:48.290
aluminum lithium mainline program against composite structure of using the same set

00:05:48.290 --> 00:05:48.890
of requirements.

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:54.000
Mike, you said earlier that composites allow you to test different complex shapes.

00:05:55.070 --> 00:05:57.830
Talk to me about the CCM shape, why this shape?

00:05:57.830 --> 00:05:59.970
When the project was kicked off, we tried to...

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.800
respect the interfaces that the Orion project was using for their crew module.

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:09.880
Now Orion at that time had baselined this opponent called the backbone and

00:06:10.350 --> 00:06:15.810
this backbone was really used to help them manage the stuff that goes inside the

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:21.400
capsule. What the composite team did is we tied our floor to this backbone shape.

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:25.380
Then the next thing we did is we actually shaped the floor to take

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:29.110
advantage of the fact that the backbone was there and what it did is it

00:06:29.110 --> 00:06:29.970
It brought in these

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:36.860
lobes, and the shape of the lobe is the shape that the floor would want to take when pressurized.

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:41.640
If we didn't have the backbone, we didn't have the lobe floor, then what happens is it

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:43.820
would be a dome-like shape.

00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:47.860
When you pressurize a dome, it wants to go to a ball shape, a sphere.

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:52.860
And by tying it to the backbone and putting in the lobe shape, we could reduce the stiffness

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:54.860
in the edges, which save mass.

00:06:54.860 --> 00:06:59.940
Now, the composites don't inhibit aluminum lithium from having a similar shape.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:04.830
It's just much more difficult to put a lobed shape into an aluminum-lithium system.

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:07.650
Mike, what are some of the reasons people use composite materials?

00:07:07.890 --> 00:07:11.600
Well, composites are known for their strength and their stiffness for mass.

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:16.540
So they're very lightweight materials and they're used oftentimes in the high-performance

00:07:16.540 --> 00:07:17.080
industries.

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:20.000
Composites are also thermally stable.

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:24.960
What that means is that they don't change shape as they change in temperature.

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:28.340
Composites are also very tolerant to fatigue.

00:07:28.340 --> 00:07:29.980
Any application...

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:32.220
where shape is of critical importance.

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:34.700
In the airplane industry, they like it on a wing.

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:36.540
They want to manage the shape of the wing.

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:40.260
The mast of a sailboat, the hull of a sailboat,

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:43.760
the racing sailboat, tennis rackets, racquetball rackets,

00:07:43.900 --> 00:07:47.940
all of those use carbon fiber because they're very, very

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:49.300
stiff, very, very lightweight.

00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:52.880
Mike, any test to failure, to see if the CCM

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:53.800
would completely fail?

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:55.580
Absolutely, and part of the program

00:07:55.580 --> 00:07:57.770
was to pressurize it until it failed.

00:07:57.770 --> 00:07:59.680
and we pumped it full of water.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:04.080
with that same set of instrumentation on there and continued to pump it up

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:06.500
until it actually cracked or popped.

00:08:08.350 --> 00:08:11.200
Now, we were expecting a fairly dramatic failure.

00:08:12.610 --> 00:08:14.950
All of our predictions were that it was going to be dramatic,

00:08:14.950 --> 00:08:17.990
and it turned out it was not very dramatic at all.

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:19.280
And is that positive?

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:21.140
That's a very positive result.

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:25.160
Now, this was designed to dock to the International Space Station,

00:08:25.240 --> 00:08:28.080
so it's designed for an internal pressure of 15 psi,

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:29.780
