00:16:34.180 --> 00:16:34.720
aluminum.

00:16:35.220 --> 00:16:40.300
With lightweight structure, you can cut down on your fuel costs, and of course one of the

00:16:40.300 --> 00:16:46.870
things NASA's looking at today is reducing the amount of fuel that's used and producing

00:16:46.870 --> 00:16:47.670
less pollution.

00:16:47.850 --> 00:16:53.170
So we're looking at the next fleet of aircraft would be lighter and more fuel efficient.

00:16:53.570 --> 00:16:54.590
What are you doing with this piece?

00:16:54.930 --> 00:16:57.710
This panel was fabricated by Boeing.

00:16:57.990 --> 00:16:59.410
We're going to do the testing here.

00:16:59.410 --> 00:16:59.970
And what we're going to do...

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:04.860
do with it is we're going to put it in this test machine, sort of slide it back, we're

00:17:04.860 --> 00:17:09.870
going to take the the platen here, raise it up, put the panel underneath it, and then

00:17:09.870 --> 00:17:14.590
position the platen so that it's just at the top of the panel. Now this machine can apply

00:17:14.590 --> 00:17:20.660
up to a million pounds of loading. So what we're going to do is bring the platen down

00:17:20.660 --> 00:17:26.730
to the surface of the panel and then slowly apply load to push the panel down. While we

00:17:26.730 --> 00:17:29.930
do that we're going to monitor the behavior of all this

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:34.470
strain gauges here to look at what the panel's feeling, and we're going to have additional

00:17:34.470 --> 00:17:39.030
measurements, so we're going to look at the displacements, how the panel moves in a couple

00:17:39.030 --> 00:17:44.270
different directions during the test. So what we're going to try to find out is how the

00:17:44.270 --> 00:17:48.870
panel behaves while we're pushing down on it, and then we're going to take it to failure.

00:17:49.070 --> 00:17:54.400
And what's going to happen is we're going to get a failure somewhere in the panel, probably

00:17:54.400 --> 00:17:59.920
somewhere in this region here, and what's going to happen is we'll see what the failure

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:04.120
load is and where it starts and then we'll take a look at all the

00:18:04.120 --> 00:18:09.300
instrumentation we have and compare that to our analysis because right now we

00:18:09.300 --> 00:18:13.560
have an analysis of the panel but the reason that we have to do the testing is

00:18:13.560 --> 00:18:19.020
we have to make sure our analysis is right and because composites are a lot

00:18:19.020 --> 00:18:24.440
newer than aluminum and particularly Perseus doesn't have that much of a

00:18:24.440 --> 00:18:28.640
database to draw from we have to do a lot of testing to make sure we really

00:18:28.640 --> 00:18:29.700
understand the behavior.

00:18:30.220 --> 00:18:35.180
You wouldn't want to put this kind of structure on a real airplane unless you can predict its behavior.

00:18:35.580 --> 00:18:43.260
And that's what NASA's been trying to do with Boeing is develop the technology to really understand that behavior so we can predict it.

00:18:43.730 --> 00:18:44.750
Well, good luck on the test.

00:18:44.950 --> 00:18:45.750
Thank you very much.

00:18:45.890 --> 00:18:47.430
And definitely, and we hope to see this on a future airplane.

00:18:47.570 --> 00:18:47.970
So do I.

00:18:48.090 --> 00:18:48.410
Right on.

00:19:07.900 --> 00:19:11.520
Okay, so far we have seen some of the ways that NASA uses composite materials.

00:19:11.780 --> 00:19:13.920
But NASA's not the only organization to be using them.

00:19:14.240 --> 00:19:17.620
Nah, there are many industries that use them too, like the auto industry.

00:19:17.820 --> 00:19:19.900
Whether it's a huge car company or a startup,

00:19:20.220 --> 00:19:22.920
all of them are looking for ways to combine strength with weight reduction

00:19:22.920 --> 00:19:24.380
to make their cars more efficient.

00:19:24.720 --> 00:19:28.200
One company called Edison II is taking that weight reduction to the limit.

00:19:28.630 --> 00:19:29.970
They have developed a car for the very...

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:34.500
light car category of the 100 miles per gallon XPRIZE competition that they

00:19:34.500 --> 00:19:39.020
feel is the most efficient auto platform ever built. This little car is amazing.

00:19:39.560 --> 00:19:43.320
It takes design cues from some of the top race cars in the world while also being

00:19:43.320 --> 00:19:47.580
incredibly safe with phenomenal mileage. I spoke with my buddy Oliver Kuttner to

00:19:47.580 --> 00:19:52.720
find out more about it. So this is the VLC? Yep, a very light car. Very light car. Tell

00:19:52.720 --> 00:19:57.710
us all about it. Well we were trying to build the most efficient car. It's

00:19:57.710 --> 00:19:59.490
It's designed to be a two or four seat car.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:05.900
And it gets 111 miles per gallon combined EPA, 129 on the highway.

00:20:06.060 --> 00:20:11.040
And we did it by basically building the lightest possible car with the lowest aerodynamic drag.

00:20:11.990 --> 00:20:16.490
The car substitutes pressure drag for friction drag, like an airplane or a rocket would.

00:20:16.730 --> 00:20:20.910
You know, these race cars are carbon fiber, and, you know, this was a race for a lot of money,

00:20:20.930 --> 00:20:23.210
so we didn't leave any stones unturned.

00:20:23.570 --> 00:20:27.140
What it really is, it's kind of like space exploration.

00:20:27.140 --> 00:20:29.980
What we try to do is to depart from...

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:34.880
the ordinary and build it very light and the auto industry has a very difficult time doing

00:20:34.880 --> 00:20:39.830
it because of all the legacies the large corporations and the huge amounts of money involved in making

00:20:39.830 --> 00:20:44.470
a major shift and this car demonstrates that the shift exists. Can you tell us some about

00:20:44.470 --> 00:20:50.110
the materials that you use to build this car? It's steel aluminum and carbon fiber and just

00:20:50.110 --> 00:20:55.770
using it wisely where necessary. It's a steel chassis car but it's a carbon fiber bodied car

00:20:55.770 --> 00:20:58.220
and the wheel centers are carbon in this case.

00:20:58.690 --> 00:20:59.550
Do you have any examples?

00:21:01.240 --> 00:21:05.220
Here's an example. The wheel is unsprung weight and it's rotating mass.

00:21:05.420 --> 00:21:11.400
So we actually have the center of the wheel out of carbon and the outer part is a cast magnesium piece

00:21:11.400 --> 00:21:13.120
and the inner part is a machined aluminum piece.

00:21:14.220 --> 00:21:17.910
All together I think this is a six pound or seven pound wheel.

00:21:18.470 --> 00:21:25.540
And you know it's an example of using more difficult and more costly materials where their payback is greatest.

00:21:26.200 --> 00:21:29.970
And in this case it was worth it, we felt.

00:21:30.060 --> 00:21:31.020
so that's why we did it.

00:21:31.600 --> 00:21:33.780
A car is a series of compromises,

00:21:33.960 --> 00:21:38.490
and you have to also balance cost in all of this.

00:21:38.670 --> 00:21:40.980
So while we all want a super hot rod

00:21:40.980 --> 00:21:43.400
that's all carbon fiber, which might be the best solution,

00:21:43.620 --> 00:21:45.140
it may not be the most realistic

00:21:45.140 --> 00:21:46.420
if you want to sell a million copies.

00:21:46.600 --> 00:21:48.820
So you put your trump card material

00:21:48.820 --> 00:21:50.260
where it makes the biggest difference.

00:21:50.400 --> 00:21:52.580
So Oliver, tell us how you got the idea for this car.

00:21:53.260 --> 00:21:54.400
It was the XPRIZE.

00:21:54.540 --> 00:21:55.940
I mean, the XPRIZE said, you know,

00:21:56.080 --> 00:21:58.620
you can win $10 million if you build the most efficient car,

00:21:58.620 --> 00:21:59.980
and then I went to my...

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:07.040
friend Ron Mattis who's a longtime American Le Mans series and Le Mans race car designer engineer

00:22:07.820 --> 00:22:12.760
and he basically reeled me down to reality there's there's no substitute for efficiency

00:22:12.760 --> 00:22:17.310
in the platform and that's what this is can you tell us how it works well in this case we have a

00:22:17.310 --> 00:22:23.850
gasoline powered engine it's it runs on e85 and but this car can be run any on any fuel source

00:22:23.850 --> 00:22:29.000
it could be a diesel car it could be a gasoline only car it could be a hybrid and electric the

00:22:29.440 --> 00:22:29.740
efficiency.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:31.660
It comes out of the design of the car itself.

00:22:32.710 --> 00:22:33.450
That's what does it.

00:22:33.740 --> 00:22:34.440
Is it powerful?

00:22:34.970 --> 00:22:35.950
It's quite powerful.

00:22:36.290 --> 00:22:38.290
It doesn't have that much horsepower,

00:22:38.450 --> 00:22:40.550
but it's quite quick and it handles extremely well.

00:22:40.710 --> 00:22:44.450
The NASA programs have been really crucial

00:22:44.450 --> 00:22:47.890
to a lot of material science and how we do things,

00:22:47.910 --> 00:22:51.730
and in certain industries they've changed the way things are done.

00:22:52.030 --> 00:22:55.440
But in automobiles, we've kind of missed the mark,

00:22:55.720 --> 00:22:59.900
and in many ways we're still building the same 4,000-pound car.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:02.350
to move the 200-pound person.

00:23:02.630 --> 00:23:05.310
If we embrace this as a method of how to build cars,

00:23:05.330 --> 00:23:08.030
regardless of the electric, hybrid, diesel, gasoline,

00:23:08.540 --> 00:23:13.020
the United States of America could become an oil exporting nation.

00:23:13.510 --> 00:23:15.670
Just embracing the principle of this,

00:23:16.030 --> 00:23:18.450
the efficiency would be a leap from what is today.

00:23:18.940 --> 00:23:19.740
Alba, thank you so much.

00:23:19.860 --> 00:23:21.740
I look forward to driving one of these VLCs.

00:23:22.260 --> 00:23:22.800
Thank you.

00:23:22.960 --> 00:23:24.490
Maybe a basic black?

00:23:25.100 --> 00:23:26.520
Next time you come, we'll have one for you.

00:23:26.540 --> 00:23:28.640
Please, make sure it's all glossed up for me.

00:23:28.820 --> 00:23:29.620
And good luck with everything.

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:30.200
That's good.

00:23:30.440 --> 00:23:30.820
Thank you.

00:23:31.060 --> 00:23:31.540
Thanks, man.

00:23:31.940 --> 00:23:34.500
As you can see, composites have really changed our world.

00:23:34.620 --> 00:23:34.980
That's right.

00:23:35.310 --> 00:23:38.550
And with NASA on the case, composites will continue to improve for years to come.

